1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,656
Seven E asks you to keep standard clinical priorities while adapting
how you assess, handle and communicate with an individual patient.
This session covers its first four sections and relevant glossary
terms.

2
00:00:13,656 --> 00:00:27,648
The school week also includes childbirth, newborn support, pediatric
emergencies and tracheostomy readings. Those are connected sessions,
not evidence that this first pediatric lecture completes the weekend.

3
00:00:27,648 --> 00:00:40,200
We will link each difference to a finding and an action. Memorizing
that children are not small adults is less useful than explaining how
that changes your airway position or interpretation of a pulse.

4
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:51,336
The examples and practice questions are newly written. They do not
reproduce the school assessed scenarios, grant a new procedure
permission or replace supervised equipment practice.

5
00:00:51,336 --> 00:01:05,664
Population reference ranges provide context, while baseline describes
this patient. Ask about usual interaction, breathing, mobility,
medicines and support needs rather than assume that everyone in a
category is alike.

6
00:01:05,664 --> 00:01:18,696
Identify the new change and when it began. A chronic limitation can
coexist with a new emergency, and an abnormal baseline should not be
relabeled harmless simply because it is familiar.

7
00:01:18,696 --> 00:01:31,608
Make a specific modification: suitable equipment, a less distressing
approach, safer positioning, additional personnel or a different time-
sensitive pathway. Explain why the change helps.

8
00:01:31,608 --> 00:01:43,536
After care or movement, repeat the important findings. Document what
was observed, what a caregiver reported, what remains unknown and why
the approach was modified.

9
00:01:43,536 --> 00:01:54,552
Speak to the patient whenever possible, not only about them. Use their
preferred communication method and allow time for a response before
concluding they do not understand.

10
00:01:54,552 --> 00:02:06,744
Explain what will happen before touching, exposing or moving the
patient. Offer genuine choices that do not conceal an urgent need for
care or promise something you cannot deliver.

11
00:02:06,744 --> 00:02:18,816
Previous frightening care, discrimination or trauma may affect
cooperation. A calm introduction, privacy and one clear request can
improve the assessment without coercion.

12
00:02:18,816 --> 00:02:31,728
A developmental diagnosis, hearing difficulty, pregnancy or body size
does not establish decision-making incapacity. Use the actual consent
process and available communication support.

13
00:02:31,728 --> 00:02:45,000
Neonate commonly describes the first twenty-eight days, while infancy
spans the first year. Developmental stage names and boundaries vary
among references and are not automatic treatment orders.

14
00:02:45,000 --> 00:03:00,648
Current M zero zero uses twelve years or younger for its general
pediatric clinical definition, with adult treatment definition
influenced by age or puberty. Individual procedures and monographs can
use different eligibility boundaries.

15
00:03:00,648 --> 00:03:14,256
That operational definition is not consent law. In British Columbia, a
minor's understanding and the statutory conditions matter; do not
replace the consent assessment with an arbitrary adult birthday.

16
00:03:14,256 --> 00:03:27,504
Similarly, a baby who collapses weeks after birth is not automatically
managed as a baby needing immediate birth transition. Use the relevant
clinical situation and current newborn or infant pathway.

17
00:03:27,504 --> 00:03:40,152
Observe a stable infant before upsetting handling. Use caregiver
knowledge of feeding, activity and cry, and complete useful quiet
observations before less comfortable examination.

18
00:03:40,152 --> 00:03:53,448
With toddlers or preschool children, get to their level and use
literal, concrete language. Demonstrate safe equipment and offer a
small real choice rather than an invitation to refuse an urgent
assessment.

19
00:03:53,448 --> 00:04:06,024
School-age children can often describe their own symptoms. Adolescents
should be addressed directly, with privacy for sensitive questions and
a clear explanation of confidentiality limits.

20
00:04:06,024 --> 00:04:17,880
These are starting points, not rigid milestones or stereotypes. Ask
what communication and positioning usually work for a child with
sensory, developmental or physical differences.

21
00:04:17,880 --> 00:04:28,080
Use a calm, specific statement: I am checking how effectively your
child is breathing. Acknowledge fear without promising that the
outcome will be fine.

22
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:41,112
A caregiver can describe baseline behavior, last intake, medicines and
the event timeline. Ask one focused question or give one useful task
rather than several competing instructions.

23
00:04:41,112 --> 00:04:53,328
Keep the caregiver nearby when helpful and safe, but do not let a
conflict prevent immediate breathing support. Obtain additional
assistance if behavior creates a real safety barrier.

24
00:04:53,328 --> 00:05:04,056
Record what the person actually saw and what was estimated. A
caregiver saying the child feels hot is important history, not an
invented measured temperature.

25
00:05:04,056 --> 00:05:15,576
Infants and young children have relatively large tongues, smaller
airways and shorter tracheas. Added swelling or secretions can
therefore have a substantial effect on airflow.

26
00:05:15,576 --> 00:05:28,656
The school and older text describe a funnel-shaped airway and a narrow
cricoid ring. Treat this as a simplified teaching model, not a rule
that proves the right tube or adjunct size for every child.

27
00:05:28,656 --> 00:05:40,296
Assess function: is air moving, is the chest rising and does the child
protect the airway? A strong sound or one saturation number does not
complete that assessment.

28
00:05:40,296 --> 00:05:52,680
Position and suction as indicated using appropriate equipment and
authorization. Avoid blind sweeps, forceful insertion and unnecessary
repeated stimulation of a small airway.

29
00:05:52,680 --> 00:06:03,360
A young infant's large occiput can flex the neck when lying on a flat
surface. The useful goal is airway patency, not a photograph-perfect
angle.

30
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:14,064
Small support under the shoulders or torso may help when indicated.
Avoid automatically stacking padding under the head or overextending
the neck.

31
00:06:14,064 --> 00:06:24,240
Recheck after any position change or move. Correct positioning should
improve effective airflow or ventilation, not merely satisfy the
checklist.

32
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:35,904
If trauma is suspected, coordinate airway priorities with suitable
spinal protection and the child's anatomy. Do not sacrifice breathing
while pursuing an unsafe forced alignment.

33
00:06:35,904 --> 00:06:49,104
The school calls young infants obligate nose breathers. Clinically,
nasal obstruction can seriously interfere with breathing and feeding,
but do not turn the phrase into a claim that mouth breathing is
impossible.

34
00:06:49,104 --> 00:07:01,056
Ask whether the infant can feed and breathe as usual, and observe
respiratory effort and airflow. Poor feeding may be an early
functional warning rather than a minor appetite issue.

35
00:07:01,056 --> 00:07:11,592
Use appropriate clearance or support when indicated. Repeated deep
suction can cause harm and does not replace effective ventilation when
breathing is inadequate.

36
00:07:11,592 --> 00:07:24,960
If the infant becomes exhausted, less responsive or poorly perfused,
escalate the whole assessment. Clearing a blocked nose does not
exclude another respiratory, cardiac or metabolic cause.

37
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:38,712
A compliant chest wall makes indrawing visible when negative
intrathoracic pressure increases. The diaphragm is important, and
normal abdominal movement must be distinguished from concerning
thoracoabdominal dissociation.

38
00:07:38,712 --> 00:07:52,512
Children have relatively high metabolic demand and can lose oxygen
reserve quickly when effective ventilation is interrupted. The
immediate task is to make breathing work, not simply increase an
oxygen flow number.

39
00:07:52,512 --> 00:08:04,032
Measure respiratory rate with adequate observation, especially when
irregular. Also assess depth, airflow, sounds, effort and the child's
response.

40
00:08:04,032 --> 00:08:16,296
A falling rate can mean improvement or exhaustion. Interpret it with
the same child's chest movement, tone, interaction and perfusion
rather than declaring a normal number reassuring.

41
00:08:16,296 --> 00:08:29,064
Respiratory distress may include retractions, flaring, abnormal sounds
or a position chosen to breathe. A child can maintain saturation for a
time while using unsustainable effort.

42
00:08:29,064 --> 00:08:42,192
Failure is concern about inadequate gas exchange or ventilation, often
with reduced responsiveness, poor air movement or exhaustion. Severe
disease may become quieter rather than louder.

43
00:08:42,192 --> 00:08:56,616
Bradycardia in an unwell child is urgent, especially with poor
breathing and perfusion. Hypoxia is important, but drugs, cardiac
disease and other causes mean it is not an exclusive diagnosis.

44
00:08:56,616 --> 00:09:08,952
Do not wait for a fixed period of apnea or for all monitor values to
collapse. Provide indicated airway and ventilation support, call for
help and follow the appropriate resuscitation pathway.

45
00:09:08,952 --> 00:09:38,496
In this original practice update, a child who was struggling becomes
quieter. Chest movement and air entry are now poorer, and the child
responds less. That combination is not improvement simply because the
respiratory rate fell. State possible respiratory failure, reassess
airway and breathing immediately, and begin the indicated support
while your partner requests urgent assistance. Do not wait for a low
blood pressure or obvious blue colour.

46
00:09:38,496 --> 00:10:09,696
For the supervised bag-mask station, select the appropriate equipment
and position, maintain an open airway and an effective seal, and give
controlled breaths according to the current pediatric pathway. Check
actual chest movement and patient response. If the chest does not
move, correct the airway, position, seal or obstruction problem rather
than simply squeezing harder. Reassess after each change; a normal-
looking monitor value does not prove adequate ventilation.

47
00:10:09,696 --> 00:10:22,176
Cardiac output depends on both rate and stroke volume. Infants and
young children may have less ability to increase stroke volume under
stress, but that does not mean their stroke volume can never change.

48
00:10:22,176 --> 00:10:34,056
A fast pulse may reflect compensation, fever, pain, fear or
arrhythmia. Determine whether perfusion and function are adequate
rather than treating the rate alone.

49
00:10:34,056 --> 00:10:46,128
Compare pulse quality, skin findings, mental response and blood
pressure with the history. Severe illness may exist before pressure
falls, and a familiar pulse range cannot exclude it.

50
00:10:46,128 --> 00:10:58,344
Search for the mechanism: losses, infection, bleeding, respiratory
failure or a cardiac problem. Support the current threat using the
relevant pathway and actual authority.

51
00:10:58,344 --> 00:11:10,200
The textbook gives a lower systolic screening rule of seventy plus
twice age for ages one to ten. It is not the midpoint of normal and
does not establish a treatment goal for every child.

52
00:11:10,200 --> 00:11:23,112
A four-year-old value above that threshold does not prove good
perfusion. Conversely, a low measured value needs technique checking
alongside urgent clinical assessment, not dismissal as a machine
error.

53
00:11:23,112 --> 00:11:34,152
Use manufacturer cuff sizing and arm circumference rather than the
older arm-length shortcut. A small cuff can create a falsely high
value and conceal concern.

54
00:11:34,152 --> 00:11:46,872
An unsuccessful attempt must not delay lifesaving care. Record the
difficulty and obtain a useful repeat or alternative when feasible
rather than interpreting failure to measure as a normal pressure.

55
00:11:46,872 --> 00:11:58,080
A child who is not feeding may have both reduced intake and increased
demand from illness. Glucose disturbance can present through behavior
and function rather than a verbal complaint.

56
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:10,752
Check glucose early in altered responsiveness, unexplained lethargy or
a seizure, while continuing breathing and perfusion care. An age label
is not a reason to postpone measurement.

57
00:12:10,752 --> 00:12:22,104
Confirm units, device suitability, sample quality and whether the
result fits the clinical state. Poor peripheral perfusion can make
capillary interpretation more difficult.

58
00:12:22,104 --> 00:12:34,440
Treat a credible low through the safe eligible pathway, then repeat
glucose and function. A corrected number does not prove the original
illness, injury or infection has disappeared.

59
00:12:34,440 --> 00:12:45,936
Small patients can lose heat during examination, wet-clothing removal
or transport. Keep the surroundings and appropriate supplies ready
before exposing the child.

60
00:12:45,936 --> 00:12:57,624
Expose the chest or injury enough to obtain a meaningful finding, then
cover again. Warmth is not a reason to skip an important examination
or hide deteriorating breathing.

61
00:12:57,624 --> 00:13:07,512
Measure temperature with the actual device and site identified.
Different sites and age contexts cannot be mixed into one universal
normal value.

62
00:13:07,512 --> 00:13:18,840
Cold can influence skin color, capillary refill and measurement
reliability. It provides context, not an automatic explanation that
rules out shock or serious illness.

63
00:13:18,840 --> 00:13:30,000
The Pediatric Assessment Triangle is a rapid visual and auditory first
impression. You can often begin before touching a stable child, using
observations from the doorway.

64
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:40,392
It combines appearance, work of breathing and circulation to skin.
These are complementary observations, not three independently proven
diagnoses.

65
00:13:40,392 --> 00:13:52,080
An obvious immediate threat takes priority over finishing the
triangle. Do not spend thirty seconds observing an apneic or
unresponsive child before providing necessary help.

66
00:13:52,080 --> 00:14:02,016
Use the same framework after intervention or movement to identify
change. A normal first impression is not a guarantee of continued
stability.

67
00:14:02,016 --> 00:14:12,912
Tone includes posture and spontaneous movement rather than simply a
strength score. A newly floppy infant or child who no longer moves as
usual is concerning.

68
00:14:12,912 --> 00:14:24,072
Interaction asks whether the child engages with the environment in
their usual way. Age, sensory ability and developmental differences
affect how engagement is expressed.

69
00:14:24,072 --> 00:14:32,136
Ask the caregiver what is different rather than expecting every child
to reach for the same toy or respond to an unfamiliar clinician.

70
00:14:32,136 --> 00:14:46,344
Describe the observation and possible urgency without assigning one
cause. Poor tone or interaction can arise from respiratory,
circulatory, metabolic, toxic or neurological illness.

71
00:14:46,344 --> 00:14:59,808
Consolability asks whether usual comforting works. Persistent distress
may reflect pain, breathing difficulty or illness, but a quiet child
may also be exhausted or less responsive.

72
00:14:59,808 --> 00:15:11,592
Look and gaze include usual attention to faces or movement and any new
vacant stare. A child with visual or developmental differences may
express attention differently.

73
00:15:11,592 --> 00:15:21,192
Do not force eye contact or label its absence as a specific disorder.
Compare with the actual baseline and combine it with the other
findings.

74
00:15:21,192 --> 00:15:33,672
When a child changes from crying to unresponsive quietness, repeat
breathing, perfusion and response immediately. Silence is not
sufficient evidence of successful reassurance.

75
00:15:33,672 --> 00:15:44,616
Listen for what is usual for this age and this patient. The strength,
quality and effort of a cry or speech may reveal change before the
child can describe symptoms.

76
00:15:44,616 --> 00:15:56,832
A weak or unusual sound may be important, especially with poor tone or
work of breathing. Hoarseness or muffling needs context rather than a
single automatic diagnosis.

77
00:15:56,832 --> 00:16:06,744
A strong cry shows some airflow at that moment. It does not prove the
child has adequate reserve, ventilation or perfusion over the next
minutes.

78
00:16:06,744 --> 00:16:17,136
Complete the breathing assessment and reassess if the sound changes.
Do not use crying to dismiss cyanosis, poor interaction or exhaustion.

79
00:16:17,136 --> 00:16:27,696
Watch the exposed chest and abdomen while keeping temperature and
dignity protected. Retractions can occur at the neck, between ribs or
below the chest margin.

80
00:16:27,696 --> 00:16:40,008
Listen for abnormal sounds and note when they occur. Grunting is an
expiratory behavior that can reflect significant lung disease; it is
not simply a harmless unhappy noise.

81
00:16:40,008 --> 00:16:48,264
The absence of a loud wheeze is not proof that obstruction has
resolved. Very little airflow can produce very little sound.

82
00:16:48,264 --> 00:17:00,408
Combine effort with chest rise, depth, response and perfusion. A
numerical rate or saturation is an additional measurement, not a
replacement for these observations.

83
00:17:00,408 --> 00:17:13,344
Look for a change from the child's usual color in suitable light. Skin
tone, cold and anemia can influence what you see, so use accessible
mucosal and other appropriate observations as well.

84
00:17:13,344 --> 00:17:28,296
Pallor or mottling may reflect poor perfusion but is not specific to
one mechanism. Central cyanosis is concerning; peripheral color in a
cold infant needs careful context rather than automatic reassurance.

85
00:17:28,296 --> 00:17:39,936
Continue with pulse quality, capillary refill, skin temperature,
responsiveness and pressure when feasible. No one skin finding
completes the circulation assessment.

86
00:17:39,936 --> 00:17:50,712
Repeat the same sites and technique when tracking change. A poor-
quality pulse-oximeter signal in a poorly perfused child is not a
normal oxygenation result.

87
00:17:50,712 --> 00:18:04,344
Abnormal appearance with no obvious breathing or skin change should
prompt evaluation of glucose, neurological, toxic and other causes. It
does not establish that the brain is the only affected organ.

88
00:18:04,344 --> 00:18:17,496
Abnormal work of breathing with preserved appearance may fit
respiratory distress. Confirm air movement and response rather than
assuming the child cannot deteriorate because they are still
interactive.

89
00:18:17,496 --> 00:18:30,432
Abnormal skin circulation can raise concern for compensated shock.
Cold, anemia and other explanations remain possible, so acquire more
data without dismissing the warning.

90
00:18:30,432 --> 00:18:40,584
These are working patterns, not exclusive boxes. More than one process
may coexist and the pattern may change after treatment or as illness
progresses.

91
00:18:40,584 --> 00:18:52,872
Abnormal appearance with abnormal breathing is a serious warning for
respiratory failure or another critical process. Provide support
according to the actual airway and ventilation findings.

92
00:18:52,872 --> 00:19:03,096
All three abnormal sides mean the child may be critically ill. Call
for help, assess current circulation and breathing, and arrange urgent
care.

93
00:19:03,096 --> 00:19:13,128
It does not automatically mean pulseless arrest. Chest compressions
follow the appropriate clinical resuscitation criteria, not a
triangle-color count.

94
00:19:13,128 --> 00:19:25,080
Do not delay indicated resuscitation for a complete history, tape
measurement or perfect monitor tracing. The tool helps recognize
urgency; it never becomes a barrier to action.

95
00:19:25,080 --> 00:19:36,096
Move from observation to the hands-on assessment appropriate for the
child's condition. Address immediate threats as you find them rather
than waiting for all observations to be collected.

96
00:19:36,096 --> 00:19:47,616
Use reliable vital signs and the focused history to refine the
concern. A PAT summary is useful but is not a replacement for primary
assessment or ongoing monitoring.

97
00:19:47,616 --> 00:19:59,712
Select the relevant local pathway and obtain appropriate additional
resources. Generic pediatric knowledge does not authorize every drug,
vascular route or advanced procedure.

98
00:19:59,712 --> 00:20:09,696
Reassess during preparation and after movement. Record what changed so
the receiving team understands the trajectory, not just the final set
of numbers.

99
00:20:09,696 --> 00:20:20,952
Weight matters for many doses, but a scale measurement may not be
feasible during an emergency. Ask for a recent reliable weight and its
units before inventing one.

100
00:20:20,952 --> 00:20:34,224
Current M zero zero prioritizes caregiver information over length
estimates when a useful weight report is available. A remembered value
from years ago needs qualification, not blind acceptance.

101
00:20:34,224 --> 00:20:44,976
If weight is unavailable, use the approved tool and technique.
Document that the result is estimated and identify the tool, color
zone or method.

102
00:20:44,976 --> 00:20:55,080
Confirm kilograms before calculation. An estimate can support urgent
care but does not become an exact measured fact because the tape is
color coded.

103
00:20:55,080 --> 00:21:05,184
Check the actual tape edition and the local teaching method before the
station. A new online manufacturer revision is not proof that the
school has that same version.

104
00:21:05,184 --> 00:21:17,016
For a length estimate, align the head marker with the top of the head
and measure alongside the child to the heel. Avoid measuring to
pointed toes or letting the tape curve around the body.

105
00:21:17,016 --> 00:21:29,544
Read the relevant color zone and communicate it clearly. At a boundary
or outside the printed range, use the actual device and local guidance
rather than forcing a misleading measurement.

106
00:21:29,544 --> 00:21:39,672
Do not delay immediate breathing support to straighten the child for a
measurement. A second crew member can obtain the estimate while
primary care continues when safe.

107
00:21:39,672 --> 00:21:50,616
The manufacturer distinguishes actual-weight dosing information from
measured-length equipment guidance. The school sentence saying known
weight determines both should not replace checking fit.

108
00:21:50,616 --> 00:22:01,776
Body habitus can change a weight estimate. Do not automatically shift
every device or drug up a color zone; use the actual agent, device and
local instruction.

109
00:22:01,776 --> 00:22:11,664
A suggested airway or cuff size still needs its own sizing and
function checks. Precalculated numbers do not eliminate the
possibility of an error.

110
00:22:11,664 --> 00:22:23,760
A child longer than the tape does not automatically become eligible
for every adult drug dose. Use the appropriate reference, real
age/weight, maximum and authorization.

111
00:22:23,760 --> 00:22:35,376
Before any medicine, verify that the indication and route apply and
that the provider is authorized. A drug printed on a pediatric tape is
not new PCP permission.

112
00:22:35,376 --> 00:22:44,184
The stock concentration must match the calculation. A familiar
preprinted volume becomes wrong if a different concentration is in the
kit.

113
00:22:44,184 --> 00:22:54,360
Separate the weight-based dose from any maximum, then convert the
permitted mass to volume. Say the units aloud and cross-check decimal
placement.

114
00:22:54,360 --> 00:23:05,016
Verify delivery and reassess the desired effect and adverse effects. A
correct calculation alone does not prove safe administration or
successful treatment.

115
00:23:05,016 --> 00:23:18,504
This is invented arithmetic, not a real medicine or a treatment
instruction. Assume a twenty-four-kilogram patient, a two-milligram-
per-kilogram exercise dose and a stated thirty-milligram maximum.

116
00:23:18,504 --> 00:23:30,984
The raw multiplication gives forty-eight milligrams, but the supplied
ceiling limits the exercise dose to thirty. Dividing the uncapped
value first would create the wrong administration volume.

117
00:23:30,984 --> 00:23:42,048
At twenty milligrams per milliliter, thirty divided by twenty equals
one point five milliliters. Keep milligrams and milliliters distinct
throughout the explanation.

118
00:23:42,048 --> 00:23:52,512
In real care, every premise comes from the actual monograph, product
and patient. The example does not validate this hypothetical regimen
for a child.

119
00:23:52,512 --> 00:24:04,296
These are the younger age bands printed in the saved school Seven E
body. Heart rate is beats per minute, respiratory rate breaths per
minute and systolic pressure millimeters of mercury.

120
00:24:04,296 --> 00:24:15,312
They are reference intervals, not an instruction to wait until a child
crosses a boundary. A child with a concerning appearance or effort
still needs assessment within a printed range.

121
00:24:15,312 --> 00:24:27,648
The school infant and toddler bands meet around one year, and other
references divide ages differently. State which chart you are using
rather than silently combining its most convenient limits.

122
00:24:27,648 --> 00:24:38,712
The school and textbook tables are not identical to the current linked
BC PEWS card. We will use an explicit comparison instead of pretending
all three are the same chart.

123
00:24:38,712 --> 00:24:49,536
These are the older groups in the saved school body. Preserve them as
school reference material, not as proof that any value inside the
interval is safe for every individual.

124
00:24:49,536 --> 00:25:00,504
Activity, fever, pain, distress, sleep and medicines affect
observations. Document the circumstances and repeat measurements after
appropriate care.

125
00:25:00,504 --> 00:25:10,704
A broad school age group may conceal important age-related variation.
Use the current clinical reference required by the service rather than
averaging several tables.

126
00:25:10,704 --> 00:25:22,512
Most importantly, interpret the numbers with breathing effectiveness,
perfusion, interaction and trend. A reference interval cannot overrule
credible deterioration.

127
00:25:22,512 --> 00:25:32,736
The currently linked BC PEWS image was actually inspected. Its age
bands and numbers differ from the school and older Nancy tables, as
these two examples show.

128
00:25:32,736 --> 00:25:42,936
The file name includes twenty twenty-six, while the printed card says
June twenty twenty-five. Do not invent a revised publication date from
a file name.

129
00:25:42,936 --> 00:25:55,584
Use the actual complete reference when needed, including age, units
and observation context. A reference card alone neither generates a
PEWS score nor establishes a diagnosis.

130
00:25:55,584 --> 00:26:07,992
Maintenance-fluid information on the lower card is not a shock-bolus
order or permission for pediatric IV treatment. Follow the real role-
specific procedure and consultation requirements.

131
00:26:07,992 --> 00:26:20,016
Use a calm approach and suitable equipment. Crying and movement may
change values or reduce signal quality, but must not be used to
dismiss an ill child's abnormalities.

132
00:26:20,016 --> 00:26:30,792
Count long enough for a useful rate when rhythm is irregular. Check
that a pulse-oximeter signal is credible and that its displayed pulse
agrees with the clinical pulse when feasible.

133
00:26:30,792 --> 00:26:41,496
Describe the temperature site, pressure cuff/site and any technical
limit. An unavailable measurement is unknown, not a fabricated normal
value.

134
00:26:41,496 --> 00:26:51,648
Repeat important observations after a meaningful change and during
conveyance. Do not postpone support while pursuing a perfect set of
numbers.

135
00:26:51,648 --> 00:27:04,344
The Wednesday outline gives a two-point-six threshold for children
over one month. The actual Seven E body gives less than four for
infants and children. These statements conflict and should not be
blended.

136
00:27:04,344 --> 00:27:19,512
Current general E zero one identifies a value below four millimoles
per liter as hypoglycemia requiring correction. A six-year-old with a
credible value of three-point-two is not cleared because it exceeds
two-point-six.

137
00:27:19,512 --> 00:27:33,816
Neonatal glucose assessment has separate symptom, age and feeding
considerations. The M thirteen paragraphs are not a new cutoff for
every older child or an ordinary PCP neonatal infusion recipe.

138
00:27:33,816 --> 00:27:44,688
Keep the school discrepancy visible and verify the real clinical
pathway. Do not wait for a lower number when the current indication
and the child's condition require action.

139
00:27:44,688 --> 00:27:55,944
Oral glucose requires sufficient airway protection and safe
swallowing. Do not put gel, food or drink into the mouth of a
convulsing or inadequately protective child.

140
00:27:55,944 --> 00:28:11,016
The current sealed intranasal glucagon monograph applies from age four
and uses a fixed three-milligram device dose when the indication and
route conditions are met. It is not an infant dose or a volume drawn
from an injectable vial.

141
00:28:11,016 --> 00:28:26,760
Ordinary PCP IV dextrose eligibility and pediatric vascular access are
not obtained by scaling the adult dose. Use the actual
age/authorization and obtain advanced or clinical consultation support
when needed.

142
00:28:26,760 --> 00:28:38,712
Recheck glucose and function, anticipate recurrence and convey through
the appropriate pathway. Adult non-conveyance criteria do not become a
child discharge checklist.

143
00:28:38,712 --> 00:28:50,376
This ten-month-old has a new functional change: less feeding and
interaction, with visible work of breathing. Do not attribute the
whole picture to a common cold before assessment.

144
00:28:50,376 --> 00:29:02,184
The observed rate and pulse add concern but do not establish the
cause. Blood pressure, saturation and glucose are not supplied in this
example and must not be invented.

145
00:29:02,184 --> 00:29:13,416
Perform primary assessment and provide indicated breathing support
without waiting to complete a tape measurement. A partner can obtain
reliable additional data while care continues.

146
00:29:13,416 --> 00:29:28,248
Ask about onset, intake, wet diapers, fever history, previous disease
and medicines. Preserve possible respiratory, infectious, metabolic
and cardiac explanations until more evidence is available.

147
00:29:28,248 --> 00:29:40,248
After several minutes the infant is quieter, with less visible
indrawing but reduced chest movement and limpness. That is not an
improvement simply because the effort looks smaller.

148
00:29:40,248 --> 00:29:53,160
Repeat airway patency, breathing depth and effectiveness, circulation
and response now. Check any available monitor value against the
clinical findings rather than allowing it to override them.

149
00:29:53,160 --> 00:30:05,040
Provide appropriate ventilation support and advanced assistance if
indicated, and maintain urgent conveyance. Do not defer action until
saturation falls or the pulse becomes slow.

150
00:30:05,040 --> 00:30:16,680
Report the trajectory: initially working hard, now less responsive
with poorer chest movement. That change is more useful than the vague
statement that the child has settled.

151
00:30:16,680 --> 00:30:28,392
A caregiver reports about one minute of generalized movements, now
ended. Record the estimate and observations rather than turning that
report into a confirmed seizure diagnosis.

152
00:30:28,392 --> 00:30:42,960
The child remains drowsy with incomplete airway protection, and a
reliable glucose is three-point-two millimoles per liter. Assess
breathing immediately and do not give oral gel or a drink into an
unsafe airway.

153
00:30:42,960 --> 00:30:56,232
The reported weight has a useful recent source, but label it reported.
Use the safe age-eligible rescue pathway and actual provider
permission rather than waiting for glucose to fall below two-point-
six.

154
00:30:56,232 --> 00:31:10,392
Ask about previous events, diabetes medicines, ingestion, trauma and
illness while support continues. One plausible low does not exclude
another cause or remove the need for hospital evaluation.

155
00:31:10,392 --> 00:31:20,256
Assume permitted care has been delivered and the repeat glucose is
five-point-one. That is a useful measured response, not proof of
complete recovery.

156
00:31:20,256 --> 00:31:32,784
Reassess airway protection, breathing, interaction, limb movement and
the event history. Persistent drowsiness or a new focal finding must
not be dismissed as merely postictal.

157
00:31:32,784 --> 00:31:44,952
Continue appropriate monitoring and conveyance. Clarify why the low
happened and whether long-acting medicine, illness, poor intake or
another process could cause recurrence.

158
00:31:44,952 --> 00:31:56,664
Give the receiving team actual treatment details and the before-and-
after findings. Do not add a dose, route or normal vital sign that was
not verified in the exercise.

159
00:31:56,664 --> 00:32:07,848
Ask the child directly when possible, then use the caregiver to fill
gaps. Preserve differences between a direct observation, an estimate
and a second-hand account.

160
00:32:07,848 --> 00:32:19,560
Use a focused SAMPLE history and relevant symptom detail. For infants,
intake, wet diapers, birth history and corrected-development context
may be important.

161
00:32:19,560 --> 00:32:32,640
For medicines, obtain the exact product, concentration, amount and
last time, including over-the-counter products. Different liquid
concentrations and duplicate ingredients can create errors.

162
00:32:32,640 --> 00:32:42,168
Do not delay time-sensitive care for a perfect history. State
important unknowns clearly and identify who can provide additional
information.

163
00:32:42,168 --> 00:32:53,544
A child's pain deserves assessment even when communication is limited.
Crying or agitation can reflect pain, fear, hypoxia or several causes
together.

164
00:32:53,544 --> 00:33:06,696
A faces self-report scale is chosen by a child who can use it; it is
not simply the clinician selecting a face that looks like the patient.
Behavioral tools need their own valid age and condition context.

165
00:33:06,696 --> 00:33:19,824
Use comfort, safe positioning and an honest explanation, with actual
authorized analgesia when indicated. Sedation and analgesia are
different goals and can change airway risk.

166
00:33:19,824 --> 00:33:30,720
Repeat pain and function after care and watch for unwanted effects.
Quietness alone does not prove adequate analgesia or physiological
stability.

167
00:33:30,720 --> 00:33:41,040
Start with age, the important change and current priority. Include
relevant baseline and the source of weight rather than presenting an
estimate as a measured fact.

168
00:33:41,040 --> 00:33:53,640
Describe the useful PAT observations and reliable vital signs, with
time and trend. A color zone or suspected diagnosis cannot replace the
actual child's findings.

169
00:33:53,640 --> 00:34:02,448
Report what was actually done, why, and what changed afterward.
Distinguish a repeated glucose value from a complete return to
baseline.

170
00:34:02,448 --> 00:34:13,344
Communicate remaining threats and uncertainties so the next team can
act. Complete the patient record from the real encounter, not from the
idealized lecture example.

171
00:34:13,344 --> 00:34:23,328
You should be able to explain why an anatomical or physiological
difference changes the next assessment step, not simply recite that a
child is different from an adult.

172
00:34:23,328 --> 00:34:34,080
Demonstrate the triangle and continue to the primary assessment, with
immediate support when indicated. Do not diagnose or start
compressions from a pattern count alone.

173
00:34:34,080 --> 00:34:43,080
Use the actual approved tape and references, preserving differences
between school tables, current clinical values and role-specific
permissions.

174
00:34:43,080 --> 00:34:54,552
Then reassess and explain the response. The next sessions apply the
same framework to older adults, larger-bodied patients and pregnancy
before the full weekend integration.

175
00:34:54,552 --> 00:35:34,896
The literal pulse page describes resting adult pulse as 60 to 100
beats per minute and newborn pulse as 100 to 175. Its stroke-volume
span of 5 to 80 milliliters describes variation across life, not a
newborn fluid dose or one child target. A reference interval is not a
resuscitation decision. Interpret age, wakefulness, breathing,
interaction, perfusion and the change over time. A newborn with a
pulse inside that printed range may still need urgent support;
conversely, crying can change a measurement without explaining away
credible illness.

176
00:35:34,896 --> 00:36:13,200
The literal knowledge-integration page contains initial pediatric
history and several recorded observations, with an interactive body
omitted from the PDF. We do not invent the missing data, operate its
answer slider or copy its case as a completed assignment. Original
contrast: a three-year-old has a calmer pulse on the second check but
is becoming harder to engage and has weaker air movement. That is not
recovery proved by a smaller pulse number. Repeat the primary
assessment, support the actual threat and report which findings are
reliable, which are uncertain and what changed after care.

177
00:36:13,200 --> 00:36:52,392
An advanced airway or a silent ventilated patient is not proof that
every need is met. The tube position, ventilation, oxygenation,
circulation and ongoing monitoring still require confirmation.
Paralysis suppresses movement but does not provide sedation or
analgesia. The source adult breath interval is not a newborn-at-birth
ventilation rule, and its generic defibrillator energy is not a
pediatric weight-based dose. Recognize the setting and use the actual
age-specific pathway; do not reprogram a ventilator or give an
advanced drug merely because the textbook names it.

178
00:36:52,392 --> 00:37:33,288
Correct one specific prevention claim: the source lists proton-pump
inhibitors as preventing ventilator-associated pneumonia. The2022
SHEA-led guidance does not recommend stress-ulcer prophylaxis for
VAP/VAE prevention; reducing gastrointestinal bleeding is a different
potential indication. This does not mean every PPI prescription should
be stopped. Patient-specific oral/airway care, positioning, sedation
and ventilator plans are supervised hospital decisions. Preserve the
real indication and hand over the existing plan rather than convert a
textbook prevention list into a new field medicine order.
